{:now [{:claim "Congruence is arithmetic in the ring Z/n"
        :modern "a ≡ b (mod n) says a and b have the same image in the quotient ring Z/nZ; addition there is the cyclic group of order n, the rotations of the regular n-gon."
        :tex "a \\equiv b \\pmod n \\iff a + n\\mathbb Z = b + n\\mathbb Z"}
       {:claim "Fermat and Euler are Lagrange's theorem"
        :modern "The units of Z/m form a group of order φ(m); the order of any element divides the order of the group, so a^φ(m) = 1. The group is cyclic (a primitive root exists) exactly for m = 2, 4, p^k, 2p^k."
        :tex "a^{\\varphi(m)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod m,\\qquad (\\mathbb Z/m)^\\times \\cong \\prod \\mathbb Z/d_i"}
       {:claim "The Legendre symbol is a character"
        :modern "(·/p) is the unique homomorphism of (Z/p)^× onto {±1}; reciprocity is the first of the reciprocity laws that class field theory and the Langlands programme generalise."
        :tex "\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}\\frac{q-1}{2}}"}
       {:claim "The prime number theorem"
        :modern "π(x) ~ x/log x was proved in 1896 by Hadamard and de la Vallée Poussin from Riemann's zeta function; Li(x) is the better approximation, with an error governed by the zeros of ζ."
        :tex "\\pi(x) = \\operatorname{Li}(x) + O\\!\\left(x\\,e^{-c\\sqrt{\\log x}}\\right)"}]
 :fields [{:field :number-theory :label "Cryptography" :how "RSA and Diffie-Hellman compute powers in (Z/n)^× and rely on Euler's theorem and primitive roots." :example "RSA decrypts because m^(ed) ≡ m (mod n) when ed ≡ 1 (mod φ(n))."}
          {:field :abstract-algebra :how "Z/n is the model of a quotient ring, its units the model of a finite abelian group; the structure theorem names every finite abelian group as a product of cyclic ones." :example "(Z/15)^× ≅ Z/2 × Z/4."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "Modular arithmetic is the arithmetic of machine words, hash functions and checksums." :example "Fermat's test a^(n-1) ≡ 1 (mod n) screens primes in every cryptographic library."}
          {:field :analysis :how "Dirichlet's L-functions and Riemann's zeta function turn counts of primes into analysis in the complex plane." :example "Primes in progressions: each residue class coprime to q gets 1/φ(q) of the primes."}]
 :tangents [{:series :group-theory :hook "Z/n, (Z/p)^× and the structure theorem for finite abelian groups are the first examples of every group-theory course."}]
